===Etymology===
From {{proto|Germanic|þurnuz|thorn, sloe|lang=ang}}, {{|x|gem-pro|þurnaz}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|(s)tern-|thorny bush, thorn|lang=ang}}, a variant of the base {{proto|Indo-European|title=|(s)ter-||(s)terə-|rigid, stiff|lang=ang}}.  Germanic cognates include Old Saxon {{term|thorn|lang=osx}} (Low German {{term|Dorn|lang=nds}}), Dutch {{term|doorn|lang=nl}}, Old High German {{term|thorn|lang=goh}} (German {{term|Dorn|lang=de}}), Old Norse {{term|þorn|lang=non}} (Swedish {{term|törne|lang=sv}}), Gothic {{term|𐌸𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌽𐌿𐍃||tr=þaurnus|lang=got|sc=Goth}}. The Indo-European root is also the source of Old Church Slavonic {{term|трьнъ|lang=cu}} (Russian {{term|тёрн||sloe, blackthorn|lang=ru}}), Sanskrit {{term|तृण||grass|lang=sa|sc=Deva}}.

